  "body": "Left from #5556 (fixed in 96f99b760, which only made the record's *write* error seen).\n\n`noteResetUsed` (internal/provider/codex_resets_used.go:90-97 at 31ece9761) treats every error from `readResetsUsed` as \"doesn't parse\": it tries to copy the file aside, then sets `us = nil` and writes the new list over the file.\n\n- A read that fails for any other reason than a missing file or bad JSON (permission denied, EIO, the path briefly unreadable, an `EINTR`/`EAGAIN` on a network home) also lands there. `os.ReadFile` fails again inside the branch, so no `.bad-` copy is made, and the write that follows replaces the whole history of resets used with this one entry. Per the code standards a failed read means \"unknown\", not \"empty\".\n- The `.bad-` copy's own `os.WriteFile` error is dropped (`_ =`), so even for bad JSON the history can be overwritten with no copy kept and no word of it.\n\nExpected: only a parse failure writes over the file, and only after the copy aside succeeded. Any other read failure leaves the file alone and returns the error, as noteResetUsed's write failures now do. A test can stand in a read failure by making the file unreadable (chmod 000, skipped on Windows and as root) and check that the old entries are still there afterwards.\n\nFound reviewing ggbdpq's patch for #5556 (reported by cgsme).\n",
